No—almost certainly not with normal unaided vision.

Although the Moon is geometrically above the horizon, it is:

- only **0.09% illuminated**, essentially at new Moon;
- just **3.45° from the Sun**, so the crescent is extraordinarily thin and buried in intense daylight glare;
- only about **10.5° above the horizon**, where atmospheric scattering and extinction further reduce contrast.

This is far beyond ordinary naked-eye crescent visibility. Even finding it with optical aid would be extremely difficult, and attempting to do so that close to the Sun is hazardous unless the Sun is securely blocked and the equipment is operated by an experienced observer. So in practice, you would not be able to spot it.

